Z||000000||theogre||07-30-2000||11:45 PM||||I'm thinking about getting one of these as a present to self once the chemo mess is done and the real paychecks are comming again. There aren't that many things you can, or need, do to the 87..... I figured a bit of improved breathing should help. Especaially since the factory manifold is cracked again.<P><BR>1. does anyone have one of the triY's on an 87+ 4 cyl? So far I don't see a number listed anyplace that is suposed to fit the 87, only the 84-86.<P>2. does anyone have pictures of one? Or could someone take a couple? any year, even an installed one, so I can see what it looks like?<P>3. how much alteration of the exhaust system was needed to install it? Does it still have the springs & bolts like the old factory manifold for connection to the rest of the exhaust?<P>4. what part # did you use and did you need any adaptors etc?<P>5. Does someone maybe have one of these you don't want any more because of an engine upgrade?<P>------------------<BR>Lake Superior? Is that what I stepped in? Sorry.||||reg||5
Z||000001||GT Bastard||07-31-2000||12:28 AM||||Hey hey hey, I thought the Ogre was a OEM parts kinda guy.  Nice to see you're hopping up the Duke.  How bout a 50 shot of NOS with that?   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000002||ggarsk||07-31-2000||12:41 AM||||Ogre,I got a picture of the Tri-Y along side of the stock manifold(84) I'll scan it and try to post it. Installation is simple,you can use the heat shield/exhaust gasket to make a heat shield for the starter. The O2 sensor goes on the reducer...I have intermediate 13 codes since I put the header on. I think it is from the placement. It would probaslly be better if you had a nut welded to the collector for the sensor. I'll look up the reducer #. I think it was $58. It feels like I've lost a little on the low end but once it reaches about 1800 rpm it takes off. I have to be very carefull not to over rev it. I think I'm going to have to got some of that header wrap,the engine compartment seems to be hotter now. You'll need to take it to a muffler shop to have the a cat and muffler fit in. The header pipes come down beneath the engine. I hope this helps...if you have anymore ?'s e-mail me.||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||07-31-2000||12:54 AM||||I mostly am keeping this car OE. I always wanted on of these since they where new so I'm making mine as "new" as I can. I figure since a new manifold is over 100$ I might as well look at the extra and stuff the header on.<P>I've looked at nitrous over the years for several cars. I'd never use it enough to make it worth it. I don't race, but having a few more HP & FtLbs getting out in heavy traffic a bad thing is not. A header really is about all you can add to the 87+ 4's. <P>(GT, Yes I knew you where kidding.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )<P>I was able to look this up more on the holley site. <P>GGarsk, Do you know what number it was? Acording to the notes, hooker # 4703 has the O2 bung on it and is more of a drop in. Notes say it doesn't need a reducer. (There's another one, 4702, that needs the 11030 reducer.) I'd still like to see the picture.<P>Yes, you could have codes because of location. Might be the O2 isn't staying hot enough. Could also be you are getting a bad electrical connection.<P>My problem still would be does anyone know if it fits on an 87?<BR><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 07-31-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||2FAST4U||07-31-2000||02:32 AM||||I've got the 4703 with the bung in the collector. The collector  makes a u bend and stops juct be for the cat. you'll still need a reducer to go from the 2 1/2" collector to the cat. It came with a starter shield,bolts, and flange gasket. I went ahead and bought a 2 1/2" Cat and Edelbrock muffler and went 2 1/2" all the way back to the Pacesetter dual outlet tips<P>I've allways had good luck with Hooker headers before so maybe it was just a fluke but the welding was so poor in the inside of the flange that I had to take a die grinder and smooth things out before installation. It does work well and inprovements are noticeable.<P>O2 sensors are always a problem with header installations. The sensor is to far away from the combustion chamber so it does not get hot enough. Adding a heated sensor will cure the problem.<P>Hope this helps and I hope you fully recover soon so you can get back under your Fiero and start having some fun.<P>Joe<BR>(2fast4u)||||reg||1
